{{also|Phlogiston}}
==English==
{{wikipedia|phlogiston theory}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|LL.|en}} {{m|la|phlogiston}}, coined by [[w:Georg Ernst Stahl|Stahl]] in 1702, from {{etyl|grc|en}} {{m|grc|φλογιστόν}}, neuter of {{m|grc|φλογιστός||burnt up, inflammable}}, from {{m|grc|φλογίζω||to set fire to}}, from {{m|grc|φλόξ||flame}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/flə(ʊ)ˈdʒɪstɒn/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|s}}

# {{lb|en|chemistry|historical}} The [[hypothetical]] [[fiery]] [[principle]] formerly assumed to be a necessary [[constituent]] of [[combustible]] bodies and to be given up by them in [[burning]].
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|year=2006|author={{w|Philip Ball}}|title=The Devil's Doctor|publisher=Arrow 2007|page=397|passage=Stahl argued that '''phlogiston''' could explain combustion, a central concern of eighteenth-century chemistry.}}

